// Regression for BUG-219 (found by localfuzz, 2026-08-22/23): a
// `parallel for` with TWO `reduce total with +;` clauses for the
// SAME variable used to reach codegen instead of being rejected.
// checker.rs's reduce-clause loop already built a
// `reduction_set: HashSet<String>` clearly intended for exactly
// this duplicate check, but the check itself was never wired up --
// the set was only ever inserted into, then explicitly discarded a
// few lines later (`let _ = reduction_set;`). Both TypedReduction
// entries flowed through to codegen, and both backends' parallel-for
// lowering allocates one local PER REDUCTION CLAUSE (not per reduced
// variable) -- LLVM rejected the resulting IR outright ("multiple
// definition of local value named `loc_red_0`"), while the C/OpenMP
// backend accepted it but the behavior was undefined (which clause's
// partial sum wins is unspecified).
//
// Found independently twice by the localfuzz harness within a few
// hours (a struct-capture variant and a plain-int variant of the
// same duplicate-clause shape), both misclassified by its mechanical
// signature as "backend-divergence" since the two backends failed
// differently rather than agreeing.
//
// Must reject — `xfail_*` prefix asserts the compiler errors out.
fn main() -> i64 {
  let total: i64 = 0;
  parallel for i from 0 to 5
  reduce total with +;
  reduce total with +;
  {
    total = total + i;
  }
  print total;
  return 0;
}
